[34m##[0m[37m [0m[34mWhat I changed[0m

[37mOnly the solver[0m[37m/linear[0m[37m-al[0m[37mgebra strategy[0m[37m — [0m[1;33m**[0m[1;33mno physical property was touched[0m[1;33m**[0m[37m (m[0m[37masses, inert[0m[37mias, ge[0m[37moms, joints[0m[37m, damping, timest[0m[37mep, integr[0m[37mator, constraint parameters[0m[37m are all byte[0m[37m-identical to [0m[32m`[0m[32mmodel_ref.xml[0m[32m`[0m[37m):[0m

[37m```[0m[37mxml[0m
[37m<[0m[31moption[0m[37m [0m[38;5;9mj[0m[37macobian="dense[0m[37m" solver="P[0m[37mGS" tolerance="1e[0m[37m-12"/>[0m
[37m```[0m

[37m## Why this works[0m

[37mProfiling (a 41-body cable = nv 118) showed `mj_step` was dominated by `mj_fwdConstraint` (~0.15 ms of 0.27 ms per step):[0m

[37m| component | Newton (ref) | PGS (tuned) |[0m
[37m|---|---|---|[0m
[37m| `mj_factorM` (dense Cholesky of M) | 38 µs, done every step | not needed |[0m
[37m| `mj_fwdConstraint` | 115–154 µs | 4.5 µs |[0m
[37m| full step | 0.27 ms | 0.12 ms |[0m

[37m- **`solver="PGS"`** avoids the expensive mass-matrix factorization/dense KKT solves. Since this model has only 3 constraints (`nefc=3`), Gauss–Seidel converges to the same fixed point almost instantly.[0m
[37m- **`jacobian="dense"`** beats the default "auto/sparse" path for this chain (dense 118² fits in cache; sparse Cholesky suffers fill-in).[0m
[37m- **`tolerance="1e-12"`** was the key for correctness: with the default `1e-8`, PGS's slightly less converged constraint solution differed from Newton's by ~1e-9 *relative* on accelerations of magnitude ~1e6 (the cable is very stiff), which accumulated to ~4e-6 in the final `qvel` — passable but thin. Tightening the solve drives the divergence down by two orders of magnitude at **no measurable time cost**.[0m

